## Residual risks (return)
| ID | Sev | Residual | Why not 2.8 FAIL |
|----|-----|----------|------------------|
| R1 | MED | emit-c / emit-llvm / emit-bc skip `check_residual_free_calls` | Direct C unknown-call; LLVM/BC unresolved CALL; **no** splice |
| R2 | LOW | `let f = macro_expand; f(...)` check ALLOW | Deny-shape gap; emit keeps STRING; gcc undeclared |
| R3 | MED | 2.10 user sink wrappers (`leak(token)`) | Separate sprint; not macro expansion |
| R4 | MED | 5.8 `${}` interp **not implemented** | Literal `"${token}"`; would become 2.8-adjacent **if** interp ships without a taint pass |
| R5 | LOW | Honesty smokes still look for `bootstrap/*.md` (`ast_macros_residual_smoke.oo`, `secret_taint_residual_smoke.oo`) | Doc-path rot; live refuse is `check_residual` + `residual_path_a_floor_smoke.oo` (green) |

Do **not** treat R1–R5 as “macros exist.” They do not.

---

## What we do **not** claim
- A macro-body SECRET second pass is **implemented** (it is not).
- Full IFC / `#[Secret]` / interproc beyond path A.
- `${}` interpolation (5.8) or `__sec_sink__` wrappers (2.10).
- emit-without-check is as tight as `oodac check` on residual names.
- `ooda` product CLI on out-of-tree `/tmp` paths (this host: in-tree
  `residual_path_a_floor_smoke` product refuse is the rail).

---

